  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nIt will be another day of hot and humid weather conditions for the \nregion today with heat indices around 100 degrees again, however \ncooler temperatures will be on the way after today. A cold front \nwill sink slowly southward across Kansas with convection expected to \ndevelop around peak heating after 3pm along the front in central \nKansas. Conditions are favorable for a few strong to severe storms \nto materialize during the late afternoon-evening time frame with \nmodest instability and wind shear around 30kts for some organized \nmulti-cell storms. Damaging wind threat seems to be the primary risk \nwith inverted-V sounding profiles and some potential for large hail \nbefore multi-cells begin to cluster. The threat looks to transition \nto more of isolated damaging winds and heavy rainfall overnight. The \nfront will continue a slow southward track for Sunday with cooler \ntemperatures finally beginning to overspread the area from north to \nsouth. There is some potential for a few strong storms ahead of the \nfront mainly for locations east of the Kansas turnpike during the \nafternoon where atmosphere could destabilize more. Otherwise showers \nand storms will gradually decrease in coverage later Sunday night.\n\nHigh confidence for cooler temperatures to finally influence the \nregion for Monday through Wednesday as surface high pressure \ndominates over the central plains. This will result in daytime highs \nactually being cooler than average along with dry weather expected. \nLong range models continue to suggest that next chance of rain could \narrive Wednesday night into Thursday for Kansas.",
